{"message":"I understand the frustration. Since mobile data is now enabled but the phone still shows 2G and the VPN is connected, the next single step is: temporarily turn OFF the VPN, then test again.

A VPN can sometimes block all traffic if it can’t establish a tunnel over a weak mobile connection.

Please turn the VPN off, wait about 30–60 seconds, and then try loading a simple webpage or running the speed test again. Let me know whether it still says “No Connection,” and whether the network changes from 2G to LTE/4G/5G."}

{"message":"Next single step: **move to a spot with stronger cellular signal** — ideally near a window or outside — then wait about **1–2 minutes** and check whether it changes from **2G** to **LTE/4G/5G**.

Because your phone is still showing **poor signal + 2G**, this looks more like a signal/coverage or network registration issue than a data-balance issue.

After moving, try loading a simple webpage or running the speed test again and tell me whether the signal improves or if it still stays on 2G."}

{"message":"Perfect. After the 60 seconds, run the speed test.

If it works but the speed is poor, try manually selecting a different French carrier network.

If it still says “No Connection,” the next step is to check the APN/carrier settings on the phone."}

{
  "message": "I understand — let’s take it one step at a time. Since it says data is disabled, the next thing to check is whether Cellular/Mobile Data is turned on.",
  "next_step": {
    "title": "Turn Cellular/Mobile Data on",
    "instructions": [
      "Open your phone’s Settings.",
      "Go to Cellular or Mobile Network.",
      "Look for Cellular Data or Mobile Data.",
      "Make sure it is turned ON."
    ]
  },
  "follow_up": "Please check that now and tell me whether Cellular/Mobile Data was off or already on."
}

{
  "message": "Thanks for checking. Since Mobile Data is on but the phone still shows no signal, the next step is to restart the phone so it re-registers with the network.",
  "next_step": {
    "title": "Restart your phone",
    "instructions": [
      "Press and hold the power button, or the power and volume button combination if your phone requires it.",
      "Choose Restart if available. If you only see Power Off, turn it off completely.",
      "Wait about 30 seconds.",
      "Turn the phone back on.",
      "After it starts up, wait 1–2 minutes and check the status bar."
    ]
  },
  "follow_up": "Please restart the phone now and tell me whether it still says “No Service” after it comes back on."
}

{
  "message": "Thanks for trying that. I checked the device record, and your phone is showing as activated on our side, so the next thing to check is whether the SIM/eSIM is enabled on the phone itself.",
  "next_step": {
    "title": "Check that the SIM/eSIM line is turned on",
    "instructions": [
      "Open Settings.",
      "Go to Cellular or Mobile Network.",
      "Look for SIMs, eSIMs, or Cellular Plans.",
      "Select the line for 555-123-2002 if you see it.",
      "Make sure the line is turned ON or enabled.",
      "If there is an option called Network Selection, make sure it is set to Automatic."
    ]
  },
  "follow_up": "Please check that and tell me whether the line was enabled, disabled, or if you don’t see the line listed at all."
}

{
  "message": "That explains the “No Service” issue. When the SIM is locked with a PIN, the phone cannot connect to the mobile network until it’s unlocked.",
  "next_step": {
    "title": "Unlock the SIM with the SIM PIN",
    "instructions": [
      "On the SIM locked screen, enter your SIM PIN if you know it.",
      "Be careful: too many wrong attempts can lock the SIM further and require a PUK code.",
      "If you do not know the SIM PIN, do not keep guessing."
    ]
  },
  "important_note": "If you don’t know the PIN or it has already been entered incorrectly too many times, we’ll need help with a PUK/SIM unlock process, which I may need to route to a human support agent.",
  "follow_up": "Do you know the SIM PIN, or is the phone asking for a PUK code now?"
}

{
  "message": "Got it. Since it is only asking for a SIM PIN and not a PUK code, please do not guess the PIN repeatedly — too many incorrect attempts can trigger a PUK lock.",
  "next_step": {
    "title": "Try the default SIM PIN carefully",
    "instructions": [
      "If you have never changed the SIM PIN, try the default SIM PIN: 1234.",
      "Enter it only once.",
      "If it does not work, stop and do not keep trying."
    ]
  },
  "follow_up": "Please try 1234 once and let me know if it unlocks the SIM or if it says the PIN is incorrect."
}

{
  "message": "I’ve connected you with a human support agent for the SIM unlock process.",
  "summary": "They’ll have the details that your account and line are active, the device is activated, and the SIM is showing as PIN-locked. Please avoid entering random PINs while you wait, since too many incorrect attempts can require a PUK unlock."
}

{"message":"No — restarting the phone will not delete your photos.

A restart is just like turning the phone off and back on. Your pictures, contacts, and messages should still be there afterward.

Please go ahead and restart it when you’re ready, then try the picture message again with Wi‑Fi still off."}

{"message":"That explains why MMS is failing: MMS picture messages typically need mobile data, and since you’re abroad, data roaming also needs to be enabled.

Next step only: please turn ON Mobile Data first, but leave Data Roaming OFF for the moment. Then tell me if your signal changes from 2G to 3G/4G/LTE/5G or stays on 2G.

Note: since you’ve already reached your 15.0 GB data limit, data may still be restricted until you add data or the cycle resets, but let’s confirm the phone can connect to mobile data first."}

{"message":"Yes. This is a one-time data refuel, not a mobile plan change.

Your plan’s refuel rate is $2.00 per GB. Adding 2.0 GB will cost $4.00, which will be added to your bill for line 555-123-2002.

Please confirm: do you authorize me to add exactly 2.0 GB of data for a $4.00 charge?"}

{"message":"Good progress. Next single step: turn on data roaming, since you’re in France.

Please go back to the cellular/mobile network settings and switch **Data Roaming** to **ON** for this line/SIM.

After that, wait 30–60 seconds and tell me whether the network changes from **2G** to **3G/4G/LTE/5G**, and whether the signal improves."}

{"message":"I understand — that’s frustrating. Since Mobile Data is on but you still have no signal, the next single action is:

Turn Data Roaming ON.

Because you’re in France, the phone may block connection/data until roaming is enabled. After turning it on, wait about 1 minute and tell me whether you see any signal bars or a carrier name."}

{"message":"Thanks for checking. Since the VPN is off and it’s still stuck on 2G with poor signal, the next step is to force the phone to reconnect to the local network.

Please turn Airplane Mode ON for 30 seconds, then turn it OFF.

After it reconnects, wait another minute and tell me what the status bar shows — especially whether it changes to 4G, LTE, 5G, or stays on 2G."}

{"message":"Yes — please turn Data Roaming ON now.

That setting must be enabled on the phone for mobile data to work properly while you’re in France. After turning it on, wait 30–60 seconds and check the status bar again.

Tell me whether it changes from 2G to 4G/LTE/5G, or if it still stays on 2G."}

{"message":"Yes, running a speed test is a good final check.

Please run one quick speed test using your mobile data. If you want to conserve data, use just one test, since speed tests can consume a noticeable amount of data.

You can also confirm by loading a few normal webpages or an app that was slow before. If the speed test looks good and pages load normally, you should be all set."}

{"message":"That’s great to hear — 275 Mbps on 5G is excellent.

The issue was the phone being set to 2G only. Roaming is now enabled, you have the added 2.0 GB of data, and your phone is connected on 5G with strong signal.

You should be all set. Safe travels in France!"}

{"message":"Great — excellent signal with 5G and data enabled means the main issue was the phone being locked to 2G.

Yes, you can run a speed test if you’d like, but I recommend first trying a normal app or webpage you actually need, since speed tests can use a noticeable amount of data while roaming.

If you do run a speed test, run just one quick test. You now have the extra 2.0 GB added, but it’s still best to conserve data while abroad."}

{"message":"Since picture messages fail to multiple contacts, it’s not likely just one conversation. Let’s check one simple thing next:

Please send a regular text-only message, like “test,” to a trusted contact — no photo attached.

Tell me whether the plain text sends successfully or also gets stuck/cannot send."}

{
  "message": "Got it. Since mobile data is off, MMS won’t send. Let’s do just one thing first.

Step 1: Turn Mobile Data / Cellular Data on.

Please leave Wi‑Fi off for now, and don’t change data roaming yet. After turning mobile data on, tell me whether the phone shows a data indicator like 3G, 4G, LTE, 5G, E, or if it still only shows 2G/no data."
}

{
  "message": "Thanks. MMS may not work reliably on a weak 2G connection, and because you’re in France, the next setting to check is data roaming.

Step 2: Turn Data Roaming on in your phone settings.

Your account-level international roaming is already enabled, but the phone’s own data roaming setting is currently off, so it can’t use roaming data abroad.

After turning Data Roaming on, please wait about 30–60 seconds and tell me if the network changes from 2G to 3G/4G/LTE/5G, or if it stays on 2G."
}

{
  "message": "I understand — that’s frustrating. Since roaming is now on but the phone is stuck on weak 2G, let’s try forcing the phone to re-register on the roaming network.

Step 3: Turn Airplane Mode on for 30 seconds, then turn it off.

After that, wait about 1–2 minutes and check whether the signal changes to 3G, 4G, LTE, or 5G. Please tell me what it shows afterward."
}

{
  "message": "Yes — please wait until it has been on for about 30 seconds, then turn Airplane Mode back off.\n\nAfter that, wait 1–2 minutes for the phone to reconnect, then tell me what the status bar shows for signal/network type."
}

{
  "message": "Yes — change it from 2G only to the most flexible option available.

Step 6: Select either “5G/4G/3G/2G Auto” or “4G/3G/2G Auto.” If both are available, choose “5G/4G/3G/2G Auto” or “5G Auto.”

After changing it, wait 1–2 minutes and tell me whether the status bar changes to 3G, 4G, LTE, or 5G."
}

{
  "message": "Great — that should give MMS a much better chance of working.

Step 7: Try sending one small MMS now, such as a small photo or picture message, with mobile data and data roaming still on.

Please tell me whether it sends successfully or what error you see."
}

{
  "message": "I get why that’s frustrating. The 5G signal fixed the connection quality, but MMS still also needs working mobile data and the correct MMS/app settings.

Let’s isolate whether mobile data is actually working while roaming.

Step 8: With Wi‑Fi still off, open a web page or app that needs internet, such as a browser search.

Please tell me whether mobile internet loads successfully, or whether it fails/loads very slowly."
}

{
  "message": "That explains the MMS failure: the phone has signal, but mobile data is not actually connecting. Since your line is already over its data limit — 15.1 GB used out of 15.0 GB — data is likely blocked or throttled enough that MMS can’t work.

The next practical step is to add data to the line. Your plan’s refuel rate is $2.00 per GB, added to your bill.

Would you like me to add data now? If yes, please tell me how many GB you want to add — for example, 1 GB for $2.00."
}

{
  "message": "Done — I added 2.0 GB of data to line 555-123-2002. The charge is $4.00 and will be added to your bill.\n\nPlease wait 1–2 minutes, keep mobile data and data roaming on, then try loading a web page with Wi‑Fi off. If mobile internet works, try sending the MMS again."
}

{
  "message": "Yes — please try sending the MMS again now.\n\nUse a small photo or picture message first, and keep Wi‑Fi off with mobile data and data roaming on. Tell me whether it sends successfully or what error appears."
}

{"message":"Yes, please try sending one MMS now.

Since it’s only showing poor 2G signal, it may still fail or take longer than usual, but this will tell us whether MMS is working at all now that the SIM is detected and mobile data is enabled.

Please send one small picture message if possible, then tell me whether it sends, fails immediately, or gets stuck sending."}

{"message":"Since mobile data is working now, please try sending one small MMS first.

At 0.05 Mbps on poor 2G, it may take a while or fail, so use a small picture if possible and give it a couple of minutes. This will tell us whether MMS is functioning at all now that data is available.

If it still fails, the next step would be to improve signal — for example, move near a window or outdoors briefly, then try again."}

{"message":"That explains it. Airplane Mode disables the cellular connection needed for MMS and mobile data.

Step 1: Please turn Airplane Mode off. After you turn it off, wait about 30–60 seconds and look for a cellular signal or carrier name in the status bar.

Reply with what you see after Airplane Mode is off."}

{"message":"Thanks for trying that. Since Airplane Mode is off, Mobile Data and Data Roaming are on, but there’s still no signal, the next step is to force the phone to re-register on a local network.

Step 4: Please restart the phone.

After it powers back on, wait 2–3 minutes, then tell me whether the status bar shows any signal bars or a carrier name."}

{"message":"Absolutely—we’ll keep it to one specific step and not change anything yet.

Step 5a: Please open Settings, then tap Cellular or Mobile Data.

Don’t change any settings yet. Just tell me what options you see on that screen, especially anything like Network Selection, Carrier, Mobile Network, SIMs, or your phone number."}

{
  "message": "Yes, that’s a good final check.

Please run a speed test now with VPN still disconnected, if possible. If the result looks good, you can reconnect the VPN afterward and test again to see whether it affects speed.

A quick tip: speed tests use data, so keep it brief. Since we added 2 GB, you should be fine, but avoid repeated tests unless needed.

If browsing and apps feel fast after the test, you’re all set."
}

{"message":"Yes — that’s a good next step.

Please run one speed test now, using your mobile data connection, and avoid starting downloads or streaming during the test.

After it finishes, tell me the download speed, upload speed, and ping if shown. Also let me know if websites/apps now load normally."}

{"message":"Yes — check Wi‑Fi first.

Please do this one step only: make sure **Wi‑Fi is OFF**.

After that, tell me whether Wi‑Fi is off, and then we’ll run the speed test next."}

{"message":"I understand — that is frustrating, especially after paying the bill. The payment did restore the line on the account side, so now we’re just trying to get the phone itself to reconnect.

Let’s keep it to one step at a time.

First, can you tell me: is your phone an iPhone, or is it an Android/Samsung phone?"}

{
  "message": "Thanks for trying that. Since the line has data available, is active, and the phone is now on excellent 5G, the next best step is to check the APN/MMS settings.

Please do this next: go to your phone’s Mobile Network/APN settings and choose either “Reset to default” or “Reset network/APN settings” if that option is available.

After resetting the APN settings, wait about 30–60 seconds, confirm 5G/data is still enabled, then try sending a new MMS again.

If you don’t see an APN reset option, tell me what phone type you have — iPhone or Android — and I’ll guide you from there."
}

{
  "message": "Thanks for working through those steps. Since the line is active, the refill is available, you’re on excellent 5G with mobile data enabled, and the APN reset/reboots didn’t fix MMS, I’ve transferred this to a human support agent for deeper MMS provisioning/network troubleshooting.\n\nThey’ll have the summary of what we already tried, including the one-time 2 GB data refill, so you won’t need to repeat everything."
}

{
  "message": "Yes — please try sending a small picture message/MMS now.

Since the phone now has excellent signal, 5G, mobile data enabled, data roaming enabled, and roaming is enabled on your line, MMS has a much better chance of working.

If it fails, please note any exact error message and whether regular mobile data works, such as loading a webpage with Wi‑Fi off."
}

{"message":"Absolutely — let’s do one step at a time.

First, please turn Wi‑Fi off temporarily, then confirm that Mobile/Cellular Data is turned ON.

After that, try sending the MMS again and tell me what happens."}

{"message":"That explains why MMS cannot send right now: Airplane Mode is ON, so the phone has no cellular connection.

First step: please turn Airplane Mode OFF.

After turning it off, wait about 30–60 seconds and tell me whether the SIM is detected and whether you see any cellular signal/service."}

{"message":"Next, let’s do one simple reset step: please restart the phone.

Power it fully off, wait about 30 seconds, then turn it back on. After it finishes starting up, check whether it still says “No SIM” or “SIM missing.”

Tell me what it shows after the restart."}

{"message":"Thanks for restarting. Since it still shows no signal/data disabled, the next thing to determine is whether your service is using a physical SIM card or an eSIM.

Please check one thing: does your phone have a physical SIM card inserted in the SIM tray, or do you normally use eSIM only?

Reply with either: “physical SIM,” “eSIM,” or “not sure.”"}

{"message":"Good — the phone is now detecting the SIM and has some cellular service.

Next step: please turn Mobile/Cellular Data ON.

After you turn it on, tell me whether the status still says data disabled, or whether you see a data indicator like 2G/3G/4G/LTE/5G."}

{"message":"Great — the phone now has cellular service and data is enabled. The remaining issue may be the very weak 2G roaming connection, which often cannot send MMS reliably.

Next step: please turn Data Roaming ON.

After turning it on, wait about 30–60 seconds and tell me whether the phone stays on 2G or changes to 3G/4G/LTE/5G."}

{"message":"Thanks. Since roaming/data are on but you’re stuck on poor 2G, the next step is to try connecting to a different French network.

Please go to your cellular network settings and turn OFF “Automatic” network selection. Your phone should scan and show available networks. Choose a different available network than the one you’re currently on.

After it connects, tell me whether the signal improves or changes from 2G to 3G/4G/LTE/5G."}
